/**
  @page guides-kokkos Kokkos local mesh
  @brief MeshBase specialization backed by resident Kokkos arrays.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-kokkos-architecture Architecture

  The optional `Rodin::Kokkos` target mirrors the core Context, Geometry and Assembly
  directories. It specializes `Geometry::Mesh` and `Geometry::Connectivity`
  for `Context::Kokkos<Context::Local, Backend>`. The mesh derives directly
  from `Geometry::MeshBase`; its topology, coordinates, optional attributes,
  and geometric maps live in the selected Kokkos memory space. The CUDA tag
  selects `::Kokkos::Cuda` and its device memory, and the HIP tag selects
  `::Kokkos::HIP` and its device memory. A native Kokkos execution
  space, such as `::Kokkos::Serial`, selects that execution space explicitly.

  There is no persistent CPU mesh and no separate publication step. File
  loading and builders use temporary host construction data, then create
  independently owned resident arrays. Copies deep-copy those arrays.
  Scaling, coordinate edits, label edits and map replacement update resident
  storage before returning. Measures use Kokkos quadrature reductions.

  @section guides-kokkos-usage Usage

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/Kokkos.h>

  int main(int argc, char** argv)
  {
    ::Kokkos::ScopeGuard runtime(argc, argv);
    using namespace Rodin;
    using ContextType = Context::Kokkos<Context::Local, Context::CUDA>;
    ContextType context;

    // Reuse Rodin construction, then compute incidences on the resident mesh.
    auto input = Geometry::Mesh<Context::Local>::UniformGrid(
      Geometry::Polytope::Type::Triangle, {8, 8});
    Geometry::Mesh<ContextType> mesh(context, input);
    mesh.getConnectivity().compute(1, 2);

    mesh.scale(0.5); // Executes on the selected Kokkos instance.
    const Real area = mesh.getArea(); // Resident quadrature reduction.
    const Assembly::KokkosIteration iteration(mesh, Geometry::Region::Cells);
    const Index count = iteration.getCount();
    using ExecutionSpace = ContextType::ExecutionSpaceType;
    ::Kokkos::View<Real*, ContextType::MemorySpaceType> distortion(
      "triangle distortion", count);
    ::Kokkos::parallel_for("mesh geometry",
      ::Kokkos::RangePolicy<ExecutionSpace>(context.getExecutionSpace(), 0, count),
      KOKKOS_LAMBDA(Index i)
      {
        if (!iteration.filter(i))
          return;
        Rodin::Kokkos::Geometry::Vector reference(2);
        reference(0) = 1.0 / 3.0;
        reference(1) = 1.0 / 3.0;
        distortion(i) = iteration.getPolytope(i).getPoint(reference).getDistortion();
      });
    context.getExecutionSpace().fence();
  }
  @endcode

  `Geometry::Mesh<ContextType>::Builder` also accepts the usual
  `initialize`, `nodes`, `vertex`, `polytope`, and `attribute` construction
  operations; `finalize()` returns a resident mesh. `mesh.load(path, format)`
  and `mesh.save(path, format)` reuse existing Rodin file formats with
  temporary host adapters. Input meshes and builders need not outlive the
  resulting mesh. Construction imports explicitly materialized input incidences
  and preserves entity numbering and ordered vertices. Additional relations and
  missing intermediate entities are computed directly in resident memory.

  @section guides-kokkos-interfaces Host and Device Interfaces

  `MeshBase` remains a host polymorphic interface. Existing iterators,
  transformations, quadrature caches, Eigen-valued points and
  reference-returning connectivity queries work through it. Coordinates and
  labels are read from resident memory. Connectivity references and host
  evaluation objects are materialized lazily; they do not own authoritative
  mesh state. These compatibility queries incur transfers on CUDA and are
  not intended for the device execution loop.

  Kernels use the borrowed device view, which accesses resident topology,
  geometry and labels without host callbacks or virtual dispatch. The
  pointer-backed view queries must execute in the selected memory space.
  Launch counts are available through the mesh's small host metadata.
  Device point-evaluation scratch belongs to the calling thread.

  Geometry must remain fixed while evaluation is in progress. Finish kernels
  before mutating or destroying the mesh. Reacquire views after mutations,
  loading, assignment, or moving their owner. A borrowed view does not own allocations.
  `flush()` has the existing LocalMesh meaning: it clears evaluation caches
  and resets attached transformations to default vertex-based maps. It does
  not transfer a host mesh. Coordinate setters and scaling perform this
  invalidation automatically.

  @section guides-kokkos-incidence Resident Incidence Computation

  The Kokkos connectivity specialization implements Rodin's existing
  `compute(d, dp, mode)` dispatch, `discover`, `restrict`, `clear`, `isDirty`,
  and `setDirty` vocabulary. Transposition and ordered intersection use
  device kernels, sorting, duplicate removal and prefix sums. Each relation
  owns independent CSR arrays.

  @code{.cpp}
  auto& connectivity = mesh.getConnectivity();
  connectivity.compute(1, 2); // Discover edges and compute edge-to-cell incidence.
  connectivity.compute(2, 1); // Reference-ordered cell-to-edge incidence.
  connectivity.clear(2, 1).restrict(2, 1); // Use existing edges only.
  @endcode

  Discovery emits reference-cell subentities in Rodin's local ordering.
  Symmetric vertex keys group identical entities; first-occurrence scans
  retain existing IDs and assign new IDs in the same order as LocalMesh.
  Ordered vertices are retained independently of the canonical keys. This
  supports mixed reference geometries, duplicate incidence contributions,
  and nonmanifold adjacency. Restricted computation skips missing entities.
  Like the core API, a clean relation is cached independently of the mode;
  clear it explicitly before requesting recomputation under another mode.
  Entity growth invalidates relations touching the enlarged dimension.

  Actual mesh topology stays in Kokkos memory throughout computation. The
  host launches passes and reads small allocation/count scalars. The tiny
  reference-cell tables reuse Rodin's existing subentity enumeration and are
  uploaded as immutable constants; they contain no actual mesh topology.
  Host compatibility queries still explicitly materialize host data when used.

  Mesh attribute and map slots grow on their first use after discovery, using
  resident kernels. Existing local IDs, labels and independent control points
  are preserved; new entities have absent attributes and vertex-based default
  maps. This is internal storage provisioning, with no user synchronization or
  publication method. Finish kernels before computing connectivity and reacquire
  borrowed views afterward.

  @section guides-kokkos-iteration Assembly Region Traversal

  @ref Rodin::Assembly::KokkosIteration follows the indexed traversal contract
  of @ref Rodin::Assembly::SequentialIteration and
  @ref Rodin::Assembly::OpenMPIteration. Construct it on the host from the
  resident mesh and the existing @ref Rodin::Geometry::Region enumeration.
  Its private device handle is captured through the iteration object; callers
  use getDimension(), getCount(), filter(i) and getPolytope(i), without
  explicitly obtaining a mesh device view. Scheduling remains the assembly
  backend's responsibility. The usage example above shows the corresponding
  internal kernel launch; iteration does not schedule traversal kernels.

  Cells visits maximal-dimensional entities. Faces visits all codimension-one
  entities. Boundary and Interface scan the same face candidates, accepting
  exactly one and more than one incident cell respectively. Interface thus
  retains LocalMesh semantics for nonmanifold adjacency. getCount() reports
  candidates before filtering, and an empty selection is valid. Attribute
  selection remains separate from the region predicate, as in existing assembly.

  @code{.cpp}
  const size_t dimension = mesh.getDimension();
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(dimension - 1, dimension);
  const Assembly::KokkosIteration boundary(mesh, Geometry::Region::Boundary);
  const Assembly::KokkosIteration interface(mesh, Geometry::Region::Interface);
  // Both counts equal mesh.getFaceCount(); filter(i) selects the region.
  @endcode

  No incidence is discovered or computed by iteration. Nonempty filtered face
  traversal diagnoses missing face-to-cell incidence during host construction.
  Zero-dimensional meshes support Cells; face regions raise an exception.
  getCount() and getDimension() use scalar metadata on either host or device.
  filter() and getPolytope() run in the selected execution space. Their entity
  results access resident geometry and optional labels without host callbacks.
  Descriptors borrow mesh allocations and must be reconstructed after mutation;
  their kernels must finish before the mesh changes or is destroyed.

  @section guides-kokkos-scope Current Support

  This specialization implements the MeshBase contract for a complete local
  mesh, including host iteration, optional attributes, coordinate updates,
  measures, boundary/interface classification, transformations, quadrature
  queries and existing mesh file formats. Missing intermediate entities and
  incidence relations can be computed on the resident mesh. Mesh loading
  replaces the topology as a whole. A Kokkos SubMesh specialization is not provided;
  `isSubMesh()` returns false and `asSubMesh()` raises an exception.

  Identity and `ParametricTransformation<Variational::RealP1Element>` maps
  are supported on points, segments, triangles, quadrilaterals, tetrahedra,
  hexahedra, wedges and pyramids, including embedded entities. Default maps
  use resident mesh vertices directly. Independent P1 control points remain
  independent until geometry invalidation, matching the local mesh. Other
  transformations are rejected without silently approximating their geometry.

  Assembly region traversal is available through KokkosIteration. On this
  mesh, `P1`, `H1<2>` and `P0g` spaces, grid functions, variational
  expressions, integrals over cells, boundaries, interfaces and faces,
  `LinearElasticityIntegral`, `Solid::InternalVirtualWork` with
  `Solid::NeoHookean`, projections, Dirichlet, periodic and identification
  constraints, and problems are assembled by device kernels into Eigen or
  PETSc systems. Rodin solvers, including `Solver::NewtonSolver`, act on the
  assembled systems. Other constitutive laws are not provided.

  The matrix can also stay on the device. A problem named with a linear
  system whose operator is a `Rodin::Kokkos::SparseMatrix` receives the
  compressed rows built by the kernels, and only the right-hand side is
  copied to the host:
  @code{.cpp}
  using System =
    Math::LinearSystem<Rodin::Kokkos::SparseMatrix<Real, Backend>, Math::Vector<Real>>;
  Problem<System, decltype(u), decltype(v)> poisson(u, v);
  poisson = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v)) - Integral(f, v) + DirichletBC(u, Zero());
  Solver::CuDSS solver(poisson); // reads the device arrays in place
  solver.solve();
  @endcode
  The coefficients equal those of the host matrix of the same assembly, and
  a reassembly with the same sizes reuses the device arrays. Such a problem
  takes no preassembled bilinear form; mixed problems are supported.

  On an AMD GPU, `Solver::RocSolverRF` (`RODIN_USE_ROCSOLVER`) reads the
  same arrays. rocSOLVER only refactorizes: the first factorization fixes
  the pivots on the host with UMFPACK, or with AMD after
  `setSymmetric(RocSolverRF<System>::Symmetry::PositiveDefinite)`, and the
  following ones run on the device with those pivots:
  @code{.cpp}
  Solver::RocSolverRF solver(poisson);
  solver.setSymmetric(decltype(solver)::Symmetry::PositiveDefinite);
  solver.solve();
  @endcode
  rocSOLVER reports neither a zero pivot nor a negative one; the solver
  checks the diagonal of the factors for the former, and a matrix declared
  positive definite must be so, otherwise the solution is wrong.

  With `RODIN_USE_KOKKOSKERNELS`, `Rodin/Kokkos/Solver.h` specializes
  `Solver::CG` and `Solver::GMRES` for such a system. Both iterate on the
  device with a Jacobi preconditioner and link `Rodin::KokkosKernels`:
  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/Kokkos/Solver.h>

  Solver::CG solver(poisson);
  solver.setTolerance(1e-8).solve();
  @endcode

  With `RODIN_USE_HYPRE`, `Rodin/Kokkos/Solver/BoomerAMG.h` provides
  `Solver::BoomerAMG`, the conjugate gradient of hypre preconditioned by
  BoomerAMG, which builds its hierarchy from the device arrays and links
  `Rodin::KokkosHypre`. The hierarchy is kept from one solve to the next and
  preconditions the conjugate gradient on the current matrix; `setup()`
  builds it again when the matrix has changed too much. It initializes MPI
  and hypre if the application has not; the application finalizes them after
  the last solver:
  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/Kokkos/Solver/BoomerAMG.h>

  Solver::BoomerAMG solver(poisson);
  solver.setStrongThreshold(0.7).setAggressiveLevels(2).solve();
  // Later steps reuse the hierarchy of the first matrix.
  solver.solve();
  // Rebuild it from the current matrix.
  solver.setup(poisson.getLinearSystem());
  @endcode

  @section guides-kokkos-mpi Distributed Meshes

  `Mesh<Context::Kokkos<Context::MPI, Backend>>` makes the shard of a
  distributed mesh resident on the device. It is built from a
  `Mesh<Context::MPI>`, which must outlive it, and answers every query in the
  local numbering of the shard, ghosts included. The boundary, the interface
  and the measures are those of the distributed mesh. The device view marks
  the entities the process owns, so that integrators visit each polytope on
  one process only.

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/Kokkos/MPI.h>

  using DeviceContext = Context::Kokkos<Context::MPI, Context::CUDA>;
  Mesh<Context::MPI> host = ...; // distributed and reconciled
  const Mesh<DeviceContext> mesh(DeviceContext(Context::MPI(env, world)), host);
  P1<Real, Mesh<DeviceContext>> vh(mesh);
  PETSc::Variational::TrialFunction u(vh);
  PETSc::Variational::TestFunction v(vh);
  Problem poisson(u, v);
  poisson = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v)) - Integral(f, v) + DirichletBC(u, g);
  PETSc::Solver::CG(poisson).solve();
  @endcode

  The `P1` space on this mesh answers its host members, getSize(),
  getDOFs() and getGlobalIndex(), as the distributed host space does, while
  the device members, getLocalSize() and getDeviceDOFs(), refer to the local
  numbering of the kernels. Single field PETSc problems are assembled on the
  device into `MATMPIAIJKOKKOS` and `VECMPIKOKKOS` objects: each process
  hands its entries in global indices to PETSc, which sums them on their
  owners. A degree of freedom fixed on one process is fixed on every process
  holding it. Grid functions store their coefficients in ghosted Kokkos
  vectors.

  Distributed device assembly covers `P1` spaces, integrals and Dirichlet
  conditions. Periodic conditions, preassembled forms, mixed problems and
  Eigen systems are rejected or not provided on distributed meshes. Link the
  `Rodin::KokkosMPI` target, available when Rodin is configured with both
  `RODIN_USE_MPI` and `RODIN_USE_PETSC`. PETSc must be built with Kokkos;
  with a CUDA-aware MPI, such as Open MPI configured `--with-cuda`, device
  buffers are exchanged directly, otherwise run with
  `-use_gpu_aware_mpi 0`.

  @section guides-kokkos-build CUDA Build

  Kokkos 4.2 or newer is required for comparator-based device sorting; Rodin
  is validated with Kokkos 5.2.2.
  Build and install Kokkos separately with CUDA enabled and an architecture
  matching the target GPU. This example selects an Ampere compute-capability
  8.0 target; substitute the appropriate Kokkos architecture option for the
  actual device.

  @code{.bash}
  cmake -S /path/to/kokkos -B /path/to/kokkos-build \
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/path/to/kokkos-install \
    -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=/path/to/kokkos/bin/nvcc_wrapper \
    -DCMAKE_CXX_STANDARD=20 \
    -DKokkos_ENABLE_CUDA=ON \
    -DKokkos_ENABLE_SERIAL=ON \
    -DKokkos_ARCH_AMPERE80=ON \
    -DCUDAToolkit_ROOT=/usr/local/cuda
  cmake --build /path/to/kokkos-build --parallel
  cmake --install /path/to/kokkos-build

  cmake -S . -B build-kokkos-cuda \
    -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=g++ \
    -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/path/to/kokkos-install \
    -DRODIN_USE_KOKKOS=ON \
    -DRODIN_BUILD_UNIT_TESTS=ON \
    -DRODIN_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF \
    -DRODIN_USE_PETSC=OFF \
    -DRODIN_MULTITHREADED=OFF \
    -DRODIN_INSTALL_RESOURCES=OFF
  cmake --build build-kokkos-cuda --parallel \
    --target RodinKokkosContextTest RodinKokkosGeometryMeshTest \
      RodinKokkosGeometryConnectivityTest RodinKokkosAssemblyKokkosIterationTest
  ctest --test-dir build-kokkos-cuda/tests -R '^Kokkos_' --output-on-failure
  @endcode

  Application translation units that instantiate device geometry kernels must
  be compiled by a CUDA-capable compiler. Rodin is configured with the host
  compiler that built Kokkos's host code: when it is not `nvcc_wrapper`,
  `find_package(Kokkos)` installs `kokkos_launch_compiler`, which redirects
  only targets linked to `Kokkos::kokkos` to `nvcc_wrapper`. The remaining
  Rodin libraries are compiled by the host compiler. Setting
  `CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER` to `nvcc_wrapper` also works but compiles every
  translation unit through NVCC. A Kokkos build without CUDA cannot
  instantiate the CUDA context and never substitutes CPU execution.

  When several CUDA toolkits are installed, `CUDAToolkit_ROOT` must name the
  toolkit of the `nvcc` that compiles Kokkos: CMake otherwise links the CUDA
  runtime of the system directories, whose `cudaDeviceProp` layout may differ
  from the headers, and every kernel launch then fails with a zero block size.
  Check that `libkokkoscore` links the `libcudart` of that toolkit.

  Place `using namespace Rodin;` inside a function, as in the examples, not
  at namespace scope. Rodin has its own `Rodin::Kokkos` namespace, and a
  directive at global scope makes the name `Kokkos` ambiguous at global
  scope. NVCC resolves the `using namespace Kokkos;` directives of the Kokkos
  templates where they are instantiated, after such a directive, and
  reports `"Kokkos" is ambiguous` in `Kokkos_CopyViews.hpp`.

  NVCC parenthesizes host expressions before handing them to GCC, which then
  rejects a restricted integral of deduced operands as the first term of a
  sum, `Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v)).over(1) + Integral(u, v)`, with "template
  placeholder not permitted in parameter". Place such a term after another
  term, or name it.

  See the upstream
  [Kokkos configuration guide](https://kokkos.org/kokkos-core-wiki/get-started/configuration-guide.html)
  and
  [source-build instructions](https://kokkos.org/kokkos-core-wiki/get-started/building-from-source.html)
  for compiler and architecture requirements.

  @section guides-kokkos-build-hip HIP Build

  Build Kokkos with HIP enabled and the architecture of the target GPU, here
  an RDNA4 `gfx1201`, with `hipcc` as compiler. Rodin is then configured with
  the `clang++` of ROCm. The Kokkos target adds `-xhip` and the offload
  architecture to the targets that link it, so only the Kokkos module and its
  consumers are compiled for the device. Configuring Rodin with `hipcc`
  instead compiles every translation unit as HIP: the host-only core then
  fails on `constexpr` functions that call host code, and Eigen
  `long double` calls become ambiguous with the device math overloads.

  @code{.bash}
  cmake -S /path/to/kokkos -B /path/to/kokkos-build \
    -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/path/to/kokkos-install \
    -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=/opt/rocm/bin/hipcc \
    -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=/opt/rocm \
    -DCMAKE_CXX_STANDARD=20 \
    -DKokkos_ENABLE_HIP=ON \
    -DKokkos_ENABLE_SERIAL=ON \
    -DKokkos_ARCH_AMD_GFX1201=ON
  cmake --build /path/to/kokkos-build --parallel
  cmake --install /path/to/kokkos-build

  cmake -S . -B build-kokkos-hip -G Ninja \
    -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=/opt/rocm/lib/llvm/bin/clang++ \
    -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=/opt/rocm/lib/llvm/bin/clang \
    -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="/opt/rocm;/path/to/kokkos-install" \
    -DRODIN_USE_KOKKOS=ON \
    -DRODIN_BUILD_UNIT_TESTS=ON
  @endcode

  With the ROCm `clang++`, `FindOpenMP` resolves the libomp of ROCm by itself;
  under `hipcc` it returns the flag without the library and the links fail.
  A PETSc built for HIP (`--with-hip --with-hip-arch=gfx1201
  --with-kokkos-dir=...`) provides
  Kokkos-Kernels and hypre as on CUDA. Kokkos-Kernels 5.2.2 still names
  `rocsparse_indextype_u16`, which rocSPARSE no longer declares from ROCm 10:
  remove that branch of `rocsparse_index_type()` in
  `sparse/src/KokkosSparse_Utils_rocsparse.hpp` and hand PETSc the patched
  sources with `--download-kokkos-kernels=/path/to/kokkos-kernels.tar.gz`.

  hypre takes the warp size of the GPU from `rocm-smi`, which ROCm 10 no
  longer ships, and otherwise assumes the 64 lanes of the CDNA GPUs. On a
  RDNA GPU, whose `rocminfo` reports a wavefront of 32, BoomerAMG then
  faults on the device: pass
  `--download-hypre-configure-arguments=--with-gpu-warp-size=32` to PETSc.

  The ROCm runtime loads its own zlib, `librocm_sysdeps_z.so.1`. When MPI
  initializes, the PCI component of hwloc reads compressed PCI identifiers
  through libpciaccess, whose `gzopen64` binds to the system zlib and whose
  `gzgets` and `gzclose` bind to that of ROCm, and the process intermittently
  aborts in `MPI_Init`. Running with `HWLOC_COMPONENTS=-pci`, or preloading
  the system `libz.so.1`, avoids it.

  Installed consumers enable the same compiler setup and link the module:

  @code{.cmake}
  find_package(Rodin REQUIRED)
  add_executable(mesh_device main.cpp)
  target_link_libraries(mesh_device PRIVATE Rodin::Kokkos)
  @endcode

  @section guides-kokkos-validation Validation

  Tests select CUDA or HIP when Kokkos has that backend enabled. Otherwise they explicitly
  select the default host execution space and exercise the same specialization.
  They compare all reference geometries and embeddings with LocalMesh,
  topology and attributes, resident updates, copy independence, lifetime,
  measure reductions and MeshBase compatibility. Connectivity tests compare
  exact discovery numbering, oriented keys, incidence traversal order, dirty
  flags, restriction and recomputation with the CPU implementation. Iteration
  tests compare every region predicate and entity descriptor against sequential
  assembly traversal across reference families, embedded mixed meshes and
  nonmanifold adjacency, including explicit incidence requirements and empty
  selections. A Threads
  build can exercise the same kernels concurrently on the host. Passing host tests does not
  certify CUDA or HIP compilation, device execution or GPU performance; those
  require the device toolchain and compatible hardware.
*/
